WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.510
Why am I here on a Saturday? Why are you carrying things? Why? Why?

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:14.610
Pete, these are my supplies – food, a blanket; warm clothing. Where are your supplies?

3
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:23.690
Pete, Pete, Pete. This could be the “blizzard of the century.”

4
00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.190
It’ll be fine.

5
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.030
How else had you prepared?

6
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:35.970
Well, I had just bought the latest weather forecasting software. So, I brought it!

7
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:40.850
Do you mean The Weather Genie Pro?

8
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.730
You know it. Pete thought it was pretty great too.

9
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:47.610
Do you have any games on that thing?

10
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.090
Yes! I have the best weather survival game. Boom!

11
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.210
Sounds fun.

12
00:00:54.840 --> 00:01:08.450
It is. But right now, Pete, this computer is a work tool. It will give us the temperature, wind speed, wind direction and amount of snowfall … in real time! Boom, boom!

13
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.570
I can’t wait.

14
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.710
Pete, we need a name for this blizzard.

15
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:15.530
No, we don’t.

16
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.510
All the great storms have names.

17
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:18.870
No, they don’t.

18
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:22.010
I know -- “The Big Snow!”

19
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.090
I am not saying “The Big Snow.”

20
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.950
Welcome to “The Big Snow.”

21
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.010
The Big Snow broke all kinds of records, didn’t it?

22
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:40.170
Yes it did. And every time a record was broken, we celebrated!

23
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:52.150
So far, in Washington, D.C. 29 inches of snow has fallen. That, my dear listeners, is a record!

24
00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:58.470
We just broke the wind speed record!

25
00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:06.790
Snow has been falling for 30 hours straight! That’s another record!

26
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:24.670
By Saturday night, stores and restaurants had closed. Did you bring enough food?

27
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.910
I thought I had brought enough food. But I ran out.

28
00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:35.070
Hey, Pete, where is my bag of popcorn?

29
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.550
Maybe you ate it already.

30
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.670
No, I didn’t.

31
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.430
I haven't seen it.

32
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.370
We had reported together for 48 hours straight!

33
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:55.710
Wow. That must have been a great team-building exercise for you and Pete.

34
00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.230
Yeah. You - you could say that.
